Conduct a comprehensive code review comparing three implementations (TypeScript, JavaScript, JavaScript+JSDoc) of a shopping cart system. For each implementation, analyze: 1) Type safety (compile-time vs runtime checking, what bugs each can/cannot catch, specific examples). 2) Maintainability (ease of refactoring, IDE support quality, inline documentation). 3) Potential bugs (identify specific scenarios where each might fail, like passing wrong types). 4) Developer experience (autocomplete quality, error detection timing, onboarding new developers). 5) Performance considerations (runtime overhead, bundle size, build complexity). Provide specific examples and recommendations for when to use each approach.

Review the following three implementations of a shopping cart system. Implementation A is in TypeScript, Implementation B is in JavaScript, and Implementation C is in JavaScript with JSDoc. Compare them in terms of:
1. Type safety
2. Code maintainability
3. Potential bugs
4. Developer experience
5. Performance considerations

Provide a detailed analysis with specific examples.

Implementation A (TypeScript):
```typescript
interface Product {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
}

interface CartItem extends Product {
  quantity: number;
}

class ShoppingCart {
  private items: Map<number, CartItem> = new Map();

  addItem(product: Product, quantity: number = 1): void {
    const existing = this.items.get(product.id);
    if (existing) {
      existing.quantity += quantity;
    } else {
      this.items.set(product.id, { ...product, quantity });
    }
  }

  removeItem(productId: number): boolean {
    return this.items.delete(productId);
  }

  getTotal(): number {
    let total = 0;
    this.items.forEach(item => {
      total += item.price * item.quantity;
    });
    return total;
  }

  getItems(): CartItem[] {
    return Array.from(this.items.values());
  }
}
```

